**File:** `Review.cpp`, line 5

**The loop:**
```cpp
for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
```

**Cause — avoidable reallocations from repeated appends:**
`out` starts with zero capacity. Each `out += p` may exceed the current capacity, forcing `std::string` to allocate a larger buffer and copy all existing data. Over N parts this produces O(N) reallocations and O(N²) total copies in the worst case.

**Idiomatic fix — reserve when the total size is known:**
Compute the total length upfront and call `reserve()` once before the loop:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
    std::string out;
    for (const auto& p : parts) out.reserve(out.size() + p.size()); // or pre-sum
    for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
    return out;
}
```

Or more cleanly, sum first then reserve:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
    std::string out;
    size_t total = 0;
    for (const auto& p : parts) total += p.size();
    out.reserve(total);
    for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
    return out;
}
```

One allocation instead of many — O(N) total work instead of O(N²).

